        <!-- 5. Why Results Differ from Banks & Platforms -->
        <section id="discrepancies" class="scroll-mt-24 space-y-4" aria-labelledby="discrepancies-heading">
          <h2 id="discrepancies-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-text-primary">
            Why does my result differ from my bank, Groww, or Zerodha?
          </h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
            Users frequently ask why different online financial calculators show minor variances on the same inputs. These differences almost always trace to 4 standard methodological causes:
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-3">
            <div class="p-4 bg-surface border border-border rounded-card space-y-1.5">
              <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-text-primary">1. Compounding Cadence (Quarterly vs Monthly vs Daily)</h3>
              <p class="text-xs text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
                Indian commercial banks compound Fixed Deposits on a quarterly schedule (every 90 days), whereas many generic online calculators compound annually or monthly. A 7.0% deposit compounded quarterly yields <strong>7.186% APY</strong>, whereas monthly compounding yields <strong>7.229% APY</strong>. Calcumetrics strictly labels the compounding cadence on every tool.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 bg-surface border border-border rounded-card space-y-1.5">
              <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-text-primary">2. Annuity-Due (1st of Month) vs Annuity-Immediate (30th of Month)</h3>
              <p class="text-xs text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
                Some platforms assume your SIP installment is invested at the <em>end</em> of the month. Because Calcumetrics mirrors actual mutual fund auto-debit behavior (beginning of the month), your capital earns that initial month's compounding, resulting in a slightly higher, more realistic corpus.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 bg-surface border border-border rounded-card space-y-1.5">
              <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-text-primary">3. Intermediate Rounding Drift in Loan Amortization</h3>
              <p class="text-xs text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
                Many banking legacy core systems round each individual month's interest and principal payment to the nearest whole integer. Over a 240-month (20-year) mortgage, these minor cumulative roundings can cause a ₹50 to ₹300 difference in total interest paid. Calcumetrics maintains exact float precision throughout the schedule.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="p-4 bg-surface border border-border rounded-card space-y-1.5">
              <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-text-primary">4. Gross Mathematical Projections vs Net In-Hand Portfolio Value</h3>
              <p class="text-xs text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
                Calculators project pure mathematical compounding. Real-world broker statements deduct mutual fund Total Expense Ratios (TER: 0.1% to 1.5%), AMC exit loads (if redeemed within 1 year), and statutory Capital Gains Tax (e.g. 12.5% LTCG on equity gains exceeding ₹1.25 Lakh).
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

